searchData={"items":[{"type":"module","title":"opentelemetry_nova","doc":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation for Nova. See the README for the full setup.","ref":"opentelemetry_nova.html"},{"type":"function","title":"opentelemetry_nova.setup/0","doc":"Creates the HTTP server metric instruments. Call it once from your application's `start/2`.","ref":"opentelemetry_nova.html#setup/0"},{"type":"function","title":"opentelemetry_nova.setup/1","doc":"Same as `setup/0`. With `#{prometheus => #{port => Port}}` it also starts\n`otel_nova_prom_server` linked to the caller. Prefer adding\n`otel_nova_prom_server:child_spec/1` to your supervisor instead, so the\nlistener is stopped with your application.","ref":"opentelemetry_nova.html#setup/1"},{"type":"module","title":"otel_nova_plugin","doc":"Nova plugin that links the request process to the server span created by\n`otel_nova_stream_h` and enriches that span with routing information.\n\nRegister it as the first `pre_request` plugin. It attaches the trace context\nthe stream handler stored in the Req, so plugins that run after it,\ncontrollers and libraries such as opentelemetry_kura create child spans of\nthe server span. It also sets `http.route`, the span name and the `nova.*`\nattributes, adds `trace_id`/`span_id` to the logger process metadata and\nreports the route to the stream handler for the HTTP metrics.\n\n`http.route` is the route template: bound segments become `:name` and the\ntail matched by a catch-all such as `/assets/[...]` becomes `[...]`. When the\ntemplate cannot be determined the attribute is left out rather than set to\nthe raw path, so the number of metric series stays bounded. Instrumentation\nfailures are logged without request data and never fail the request.","ref":"otel_nova_plugin.html"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_plugin.plugin_info/0","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_plugin.html#plugin_info/0"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_plugin.post_request/4","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_plugin.html#post_request/4"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_plugin.pre_request/4","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_plugin.html#pre_request/4"},{"type":"module","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter","doc":"Metric exporter that renders OpenTelemetry metrics in the Prometheus text format.\n\nConfigure it as the exporter of an `otel_metric_reader` and serve the result\nwith `otel_nova_prom_server` (or `otel_nova_prom_handler` on a listener of your\nown). Delta datapoints are accumulated into cumulative values, cumulative\ndatapoints replace the stored value, so the output is correct for either\nreader temporality.\n\nEach metric keeps at most 2000 series. Datapoints for further attribute sets\nare added to one overflow series labelled `otel_metric_overflow=\"true\"`, so\nan unexpected source of label values cannot grow the table without bound.\nLabel values that are not valid UTF-8 are rendered as `_INVALID_UTF8`.\n\nThe table is written only by the process that called `init/1`, which is the\nmetric reader.\n\nImplements the exporter API of both opentelemetry_experimental 0.6\n(`export/3`, `shutdown/1`) and 0.5 (`export/4`, `shutdown/1`).","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.export/3","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#export/3"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.export/4","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#export/4"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.force_flush/0","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#force_flush/0"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.get_metrics/0","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#get_metrics/0"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.init/1","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.shutdown/0","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#shutdown/0"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.shutdown/1","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_exporter.html#shutdown/1"},{"type":"module","title":"otel_nova_prom_handler","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_handler.html"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_handler.init/2","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_handler.html#init/2"},{"type":"module","title":"otel_nova_prom_server","doc":"HTTP listener serving `otel_nova_prom_exporter` output on `/metrics`.\n\nStart it from your supervision tree with `child_spec/1` so the listener is\nstopped together with your application:\n\n```erlang\ninit([]) ->\n    Children = [otel_nova_prom_server:child_spec(#{port => 9464})],\n    {ok, {#{strategy => one_for_one}, Children}}.\n```\n\nThe listener binds `127.0.0.1` and speaks HTTP/1.1 only, and `/metrics` has\nno authentication. Set `ip` (for example `{0, 0, 0, 0}`) to let a scraper on\nanother host reach it, and restrict who can connect at the network level.","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.child_spec/1","doc":"Child specification for running the server under a supervisor.","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.handle_call/3","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#handle_call/3"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.handle_cast/2","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#handle_cast/2"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.handle_info/2","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#handle_info/2"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.init/1","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the server and its listener. `port` defaults to 9464 and `ip` to `{127, 0, 0, 1}`.","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.stop/0","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#stop/0"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_prom_server.terminate/2","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_prom_server.html#terminate/2"},{"type":"module","title":"otel_nova_sampler","doc":"Sampler that drops server spans for excluded requests, such as health checks\nand Prometheus scrapes.\n\nConfigure it with a list of `\"METHOD /path\"` binaries:\n\n```erlang\n{opentelemetry, [\n    {sampler, {parent_based, #{root => {otel_nova_sampler, [~\"GET /metrics\", ~\"GET /healthz\"]}}}}\n]}\n```\n\nThe decision is made when `otel_nova_stream_h` starts the server span, before\nNova has routed the request, so the route template is not known yet. An entry\nis compared with the request method and the raw request path (the\n`http.request.method` and `url.path` attributes, without the query string),\nnot with a route template: `~\"GET /users/:id\"` does not match `/users/42`.\nAn entry that equals the span name also matches, for server spans started\nelsewhere with a final name.\n\nOnly server spans are dropped. Wrapped in `parent_based`, it decides for root\nspans only; a request with a sampled `traceparent` follows its parent.","ref":"otel_nova_sampler.html"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_sampler.description/1","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_sampler.html#description/1"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_sampler.setup/1","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_sampler.html#setup/1"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_sampler.should_sample/7","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_sampler.html#should_sample/7"},{"type":"module","title":"otel_nova_stream_h","doc":"Cowboy stream handler that creates the HTTP server span and records the HTTP\nserver metrics.\n\nCowboy runs stream handlers in the connection process, while Nova middleware,\nplugins and controllers run in a separate request process. The handler\ntherefore never touches the connection process context. It stores the request\ncontext in the Req under `otel_nova_ctx`, and `otel_nova_plugin` attaches it in\nthe request process, so spans created there are children of the server span.\n\nInstrumentation failures are logged without request data and never crash the\nstream, and the server span is started even when building its attributes\nfails.\n\nOptions, read from the `opentelemetry_nova` application environment:\n\n* `trust_forwarded_for` (default `false`): take `client.address` from the\n  first `X-Forwarded-For` entry or the `Forwarded` header when it is a valid\n  IP address. Otherwise it is the peer address.\n* `metric_server_hosts` (default unset): a list of hosts recorded as the\n  `server.address` metric attribute, together with the listener port as\n  `server.port`. Other hosts are recorded as `_OTHER`. When unset, neither\n  attribute is recorded on metrics.\n* `trust_incoming_context` (default `true`): continue the trace context and\n  baggage the client sends. When `false` the server span starts a new trace\n  and links to the remote parent.\n\nThe method is normalised to one of the nine standard methods or `_OTHER`, on\nboth spans and metrics. Spans keep the sent value as\n`http.request.method_original` when it differs.","ref":"otel_nova_stream_h.html"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_stream_h.data/4","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_stream_h.html#data/4"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_stream_h.early_error/5","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_stream_h.html#early_error/5"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_stream_h.info/3","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_stream_h.html#info/3"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_stream_h.init/3","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_stream_h.html#init/3"},{"type":"function","title":"otel_nova_stream_h.terminate/3","doc":"","ref":"otel_nova_stream_h.html#terminate/3"},{"type":"extras","title":"opentelemetry_nova","doc":"# opentelemetry_nova\n\nOpenTelemetry instrumentation for the [Nova](https://github.com/novaframework/nova) web framework.\n\nAutomatic HTTP request tracing and metrics following the [OTel HTTP semantic conventions](https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/http/).","ref":"readme.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Installation - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Add the library, the OpenTelemetry SDK and an exporter to your dependencies in `rebar.config`:\n\n```erlang\n{deps, [\n    {opentelemetry_nova, \"~> 0.4\"},\n    {opentelemetry, \"~> 1.7\"},\n    {opentelemetry_experimental, \"~> 0.6\"},\n    {opentelemetry_exporter, \"~> 1.11\"}\n]}.\n```\n\nList `opentelemetry_exporter`, `opentelemetry_experimental` and `opentelemetry`\nbefore your own application in the release (or in `applications` of your\n`.app.src`) so the SDK is running before the first request.","ref":"readme.html#installation"},{"type":"extras","title":"Upgrading from 0.4 - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"These defaults and values change. Check your scrape config, dashboards and\nalerts:\n\n- `otel_nova_prom_server` binds `127.0.0.1` instead of all interfaces. A\n  Prometheus that scrapes the pod IP in Kubernetes gets connection refused.\n  Set `ip => {0, 0, 0, 0}` and add a `NetworkPolicy` (see\n  [Prometheus metrics](#prometheus-metrics)). The listener also no longer\n  accepts h2c.\n- `X-Forwarded-For` and `Forwarded` are no longer trusted: `client.address` is\n  the peer address unless you set `trust_forwarded_for`.\n- `server.address` and `server.port` are no longer on metrics unless you set\n  `metric_server_hosts`. Queries and alerts that group by them need updating.\n- Methods outside the nine standard ones (`GET`, `HEAD`, `POST`, `PUT`,\n  `DELETE`, `CONNECT`, `OPTIONS`, `TRACE`, `PATCH`) become `_OTHER` on spans\n  and metrics. Spans keep the sent value in `http.request.method_original`.\n- `http.route` values change: they are route templates (`/users/:id`,\n  `/assets/[...]`) instead of raw paths, and the attribute is absent when the\n  route cannot be determined.\n- `otel_nova_sampler` exclusions match the method plus the raw request path\n  (`~\"GET /metrics\"`), not the span name.","ref":"readme.html#upgrading-from-0-4"},{"type":"extras","title":"Setup - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Tracing needs three pieces: the stream handler, the plugin and a call to\n`setup/0`.","ref":"readme.html#setup"},{"type":"extras","title":"1. Stream handler and plugin - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"```erlang\n{nova, [\n    {cowboy_configuration, #{\n        port => 8080,\n        stream_handlers => [\n            otel_nova_stream_h,\n            nova_stream_h,\n            cowboy_compress_h,\n            cowboy_stream_h\n        ]\n    }},\n    {plugins, [\n        {pre_request, otel_nova_plugin, #{}},\n        {pre_request, nova_request_plugin, #{decode_json_body => true}}\n    ]}\n]}\n```\n\n`otel_nova_stream_h` must come first among the stream handlers. It starts the\nserver span, records the HTTP metrics and ends the span when the stream\nterminates.\n\n`otel_nova_plugin` must be the first `pre_request` plugin. Cowboy runs stream\nhandlers in the connection process and Nova runs plugins and controllers in a\nseparate request process. The stream handler passes the trace context to the\nrequest process in the Req, and the plugin attaches it there, so every span\ncreated afterwards (in later plugins, security functions, controllers, or by\nlibraries such as `opentelemetry_kura`) is a child of the server span. The\nplugin also names the span after the route, adds the `nova.*` attributes, puts\n`trace_id` and `span_id` in the logger process metadata and reports the route\nfor the metrics. Plugins listed before it run without a trace context.\n\nA route group that declares its own `plugins` does not get the global list\nunder Nova's default `plugin_strategy` (`local_or_global`), so its requests\nlose the trace linkage. Either list `otel_nova_plugin` first in that group's\n`plugins`, or, for an application mounted through `nova_apps`, set\n`plugin_strategy => global_first` (Nova 0.17.0 and later) so the global\nplugins run before the group's own:\n\n```erlang\n{nova_apps, [{my_admin_app, #{prefix => \"/admin\", plugin_strategy => global_first}}]}\n```\n\n`otel_nova_plugin` never fails a request: if its instrumentation crashes it\nlogs `otel_nova_instrumentation_failed` without request data and lets the\nrequest continue.","ref":"readme.html#1-stream-handler-and-plugin"},{"type":"extras","title":"2. Instruments - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Call `setup/0` from your application's `start/2` to create the metric\ninstruments:\n\n```erlang\nstart(_Type, _Args) ->\n    ok = opentelemetry_nova:setup(),\n    my_app_sup:start_link().\n```","ref":"readme.html#2-instruments"},{"type":"extras","title":"3. SDK configuration - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Traces, exported over OTLP:\n\n```erlang\n{opentelemetry, [\n    {span_processor, batch},\n    {traces_exporter, otlp}\n]},\n{opentelemetry_exporter, [\n    {otlp_protocol, http_protobuf},\n    {otlp_endpoint, \"http://localhost:4318\"}\n]}\n```","ref":"readme.html#3-sdk-configuration"},{"type":"extras","title":"4. Options - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Set these in the `opentelemetry_nova` application environment:\n\n```erlang\n{opentelemetry_nova, [\n    {trust_forwarded_for, false},\n    {metric_server_hosts, [~\"api.example.com\"]},\n    {trust_incoming_context, true}\n]}\n```\n\n| Option | Default | Effect |\n|--------|---------|--------|\n| `trust_forwarded_for` | `false` | Take `client.address` from the first `X-Forwarded-For` entry, or the `Forwarded` header, when it is a valid IP address. Otherwise `client.address` is the peer address. |\n| `metric_server_hosts` | unset | Record `server.address` and `server.port` on metrics. Hosts on the list are recorded as sent (Cowboy lowercases them), others as `_OTHER`, and the port is the listener's. When unset, metrics carry neither attribute. |\n| `trust_incoming_context` | `true` | Continue the trace context and baggage the client sends. Set it to `false` on a public edge: the server span then starts a new trace and links to the remote parent. |\n\nEnable `trust_forwarded_for` only behind a proxy that replaces these headers\nwith its own value. Many proxies append to `X-Forwarded-For` instead (nginx\nwith `$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for`, Traefik, ingress-nginx), so the first entry\nis still whatever the client sent, and `client.address` stays\nclient-controlled.\n\nWith `trust_incoming_context` at its default, a client that sends a sampled\n`traceparent` decides the sampling of its own request, including requests\nexcluded by `otel_nova_sampler` under `parent_based`, and its `baggage` is\npropagated to every service the request reaches. On an edge that faces\nuntrusted clients, set it to `false`.","ref":"readme.html#4-options"},{"type":"extras","title":"Excluding requests from tracing - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"`otel_nova_sampler` drops the server span of excluded requests:\n\n```erlang\n{opentelemetry, [\n    {sampler, {parent_based, #{root => {otel_nova_sampler, [~\"GET /metrics\", ~\"GET /healthz\"]}}}}\n]}\n```\n\nThe sampler decides when the server span starts, before Nova has routed the\nrequest. Entries are `\"METHOD /path\"` and match the raw request path (without\nthe query string), not the route template, so `~\"GET /users/:id\"` does not match\n`/users/42`. With `parent_based`, a request carrying a sampled `traceparent`\nfollows its parent, unless `trust_incoming_context` is `false`.","ref":"readme.html#excluding-requests-from-tracing"},{"type":"extras","title":"Prometheus metrics - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Configure a metric reader that exports to `otel_nova_prom_exporter`:\n\n```erlang\n{opentelemetry_experimental, [\n    {readers, [\n        #{\n            module => otel_metric_reader,\n            config => #{\n                export_interval_ms => 10000,\n                exporter => {otel_nova_prom_exporter, #{}}\n            }\n        }\n    ]}\n]}\n```\n\nThen serve `/metrics` from your supervision tree:\n\n```erlang\ninit([]) ->\n    Children = [otel_nova_prom_server:child_spec(#{port => 9464})],\n    {ok, {#{strategy => one_for_one}, Children}}.\n```\n\nThe endpoint shows the values of the last reader collection, so it lags by at\nmost `export_interval_ms`. The exporter turns delta datapoints into cumulative\nvalues and stores cumulative datapoints as they are, so it is correct for either\nreader temporality.\n\nThe listener binds `127.0.0.1` and speaks HTTP/1.1 only, and `/metrics` has no\nauthentication. To let a scraper on another host reach it, set `ip`, for\nexample in Kubernetes:\n\n```erlang\notel_nova_prom_server:child_spec(#{port => 9464, ip => {0, 0, 0, 0}})\n```\n\nand restrict who can connect with a `NetworkPolicy` that only admits the\nPrometheus pods to port 9464:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: my-app-metrics\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: my-app\n  policyTypes: [Ingress]\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - namespaceSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring\n      ports:\n        - port: 9464\n```\n\nA policy that selects the pods replaces the default allow-all for them, so\nlist your application's own port in another `ingress` rule as well.\n\nEach metric keeps at most 2000 series. Further attribute sets are added to one\noverflow series labelled `otel_metric_overflow=\"true\"`. Label values that are\nnot valid UTF-8 are rendered as `_INVALID_UTF8`, so the output always parses.\n\n`opentelemetry_nova:setup(#{prometheus => #{port => 9464}})` still works and\nstarts the server linked to the caller, but the supervised child is\npreferred: it stops the listener with your application.","ref":"readme.html#prometheus-metrics"},{"type":"extras","title":"Span Attributes - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"Server spans are created for every HTTP request with the following attributes:\n\n| Attribute | Source |\n|-----------|--------|\n| `http.request.method` | Request method, or `_OTHER` if it is not one of the nine standard methods |\n| `http.request.method_original` | Request method as sent, when it differs from `http.request.method` |\n| `http.response.status_code` | Response status code |\n| `http.route` | Matched route template (e.g. `/users/:id`) |\n| `url.path` | Request path |\n| `url.scheme` | Request scheme |\n| `server.address` | Host header |\n| `server.port` | Server port |\n| `client.address` | Peer IP, or the `X-Forwarded-For` / `Forwarded` address with `trust_forwarded_for` |\n| `network.peer.address` | Peer IP address |\n| `network.peer.port` | Peer port |\n| `network.protocol.version` | HTTP version (`1.0`, `1.1`, `2`, `3`) |\n| `user_agent.original` | User-Agent header |\n| `error.type` | Status code as string on 5xx errors |\n| `nova.app` | Nova application name |\n| `nova.controller` | Controller module |\n| `nova.action` | Action function |\n\nSpan names follow the `{METHOD} {route}` convention (e.g. `GET /users/:id`).\nFor an `_OTHER` method the method part is `HTTP`.\n\n`http.route` is the route template, never the raw path. Bound segments become\n`:name`, and the tail matched by a catch-all route, including Nova static\nroutes such as `/assets/[...]`, becomes `[...]`, so every file under `/assets`\nshares one label. When the template cannot be determined, `http.route` is left\nout and the span keeps the name `HTTP {METHOD}`. This keeps the number of metric\nseries bounded by the route table.\n\nNotes:\n\n- `client.address` uses `X-Forwarded-For` or `Forwarded` only when\n  `trust_forwarded_for` is enabled, and only when the address in it is a valid\n  IP address. See [Options](#4-options).\n- `http.route` is rebuilt from the request path and the binding values, since\n  Nova does not hand the matched route template to plugins. Two kinds of path\n  get a wrong label: a literal segment equal to a binding value (`/users/users`\n  on `/users/:id` becomes `/:id/:id`), and two bindings with the same value\n  (`/teams/7/users/7` on `/teams/:team/users/:user` shows one of the two names\n  in both places). The labels stay bounded; only their text is wrong.\n- The trace context is not detached on a WebSocket upgrade. This is harmless.\n  Over HTTP/1.1 the WebSocket runs in the connection process, which never has\n  the context attached. Over HTTP/2 it keeps running in the request process,\n  so spans the WebSocket handler creates are children of the upgrade request's\n  server span.\n- With `cowboy_compress_h` after `otel_nova_stream_h`, the response body size\n  is measured before compression.","ref":"readme.html#span-attributes"},{"type":"extras","title":"Metrics - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"| Metric | Type | Attributes |\n|--------|------|------------|\n| `http.server.request.duration` | Histogram (seconds) | method, scheme, status, route, error.type |\n| `http.server.active_requests` | UpDown Counter (Prometheus gauge of in-flight requests) | method, scheme |\n| `http.server.request.body.size` | Histogram (bytes) | method, scheme, status, route, error.type |\n| `http.server.response.body.size` | Histogram (bytes) | method, scheme, status, route, error.type |\n\nEvery attribute value is bounded: the method is one of the nine standard\nmethods or `_OTHER`, the scheme is `http`, `https` or `_OTHER`, and the route\nis a template. `server.address` and `server.port` are added only when\n`metric_server_hosts` is set.","ref":"readme.html#metrics"},{"type":"extras","title":"Components - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"- **`otel_nova_stream_h`**: Cowboy stream handler for the server span and the HTTP metrics\n- **`otel_nova_plugin`**: Nova plugin that attaches the trace context in the request process and adds route and controller info\n- **`otel_nova_prom_exporter`**: metric exporter rendering the Prometheus text format\n- **`otel_nova_prom_server`**: HTTP listener for Prometheus scraping\n- **`otel_nova_sampler`**: sampler that drops server spans of excluded requests","ref":"readme.html#components"},{"type":"extras","title":"License - opentelemetry_nova","doc":"MIT","ref":"readme.html#license"},{"type":"extras","title":"LICENSE","doc":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2019 Daniel Widgren\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.","ref":"license.html"}],"proglang":"erlang","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.3"},"content_type":"text/markdown"}